{{Japanese name|[[Honda clan|Honda]]}}

{{nihongo|'''Honda Shigetomo'''|本多重朝|Honda Shigetomo|extra=October 10, 1582 – June 18, 1625}} was the second [[shogun]] of Mitoyagi, which now forms the state of [[Okaiken]]. He played an instrumental role on the political dynamics of Mitoyagi during his lifetime, such as establishing Okaiken's first rice futures index, now part of the Sankei 325 Index; the construction and completion of the Hokuto Omi - Shinshu road, which made it easier for the island of Harujima to traverse length of the island; the reforms of the ''han'' system and the ''kokudaka'' system, which enabled a more efficient outflow of goods and materials from each ''han''. The latter reforms, which were promoted by him as tool of the legitimacy of, caused intrigue from the ruling ''fudai'' and ''tozama'' daimyos, which later led to the Kinzō Incidents of 1625.

During the Kinzō Incidents of 1625, one of Honda Shigetomo's closest ''fudai'' daimyos, Ii Naotaka, attacked the residence of Shigetomo's father, [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Shigetada]], resulting in the former emperor's death. Much to his displeasure of failing to protect his parent as well as the failure of securing reforms and the collapse of the relationship between the him and Ii Naotaka, he became depressed and unpredictable; he invaded the Ii territory in Northwest Yagikoshu on June 8, 1625. During the closing stages of the battle, which lasted until the 18th of June, he was mortally wounded by one of the Ii's musketeers. What happened in the aftermath of the incident culminated into a series of events now called as "[[The Battle of Ichijoudani|Great Reconciliation]]", which pardoned the Ii family and securing their place in Sanese politics during the Honda Period. After his death, Shigetomo became the so-called "God of Seals".

==Early life==
Honda Shigetomo was born on 10 October 1582, in the castle town of [[Suemori]], a mountain district of [[Shirakawa]] in [[Nagayama Prefecture]] on the island of [[Yagikoshu]]. The Honda stronghold at Suemori was a mountain castle perched on a slope overlooking the Suemori River valley below. He was the second son of Honda Shigetada and his primary wife, Hatsu-no-kata. He was the full younger brother of Nabenosuke (the future [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Emperor Daigo]]) and the older half-brother of [[Honda Shigetoki|Shigetoki]], who was born to a concubine just three months later. Shigetomo's childhood name was {{nihongo|Naiki|内記}}, meaning inner record. While his older brother was shielded from the martial aspects of their clan to pursue scholarly and diplomatic training, Shigetomo was subjected to a grueling military education from a young age, destined to serve as the military vanguard of the Honda family.

His early life was defined by the chaotic political landscape of the Ogasawara clan's unification attempts. Following his father's devastating defeat during the First Invasion of Sanzu in 1590, a furious [[Ogasawara Terumasa]] severely punished the Honda clan. The eight-year-old Shigetomo, along with his mother and siblings, was forcibly relocated to the newly completed Otomo Castle to serve as political hostages, ensuring Shigetada's continued compliance.

In the opening pages of the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'', the official record of the Honda clan,<ref name="Mitsukoji">''Mitsukoji: The Official Biographical Record of the Honda Family''. Compiled by the Honda Shogunate Historiographical Bureau, 1680. Heianji Temple Archives, Okami.</ref> it is noted that during this captivity at Otomo Castle, Shigetomo met another young hostage: Manchiyo, the son of the disgraced ronin [[Ii Naomasa]]. The two boys bonded during their imprisonment, forging a complex relationship that would define the rest of their lives. Shigetomo and his family remained hostages until March 1592, when [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] betrayed Ogasawara Terumasa, allowing the Honda forces to assault Otomo Castle and free them. During his formative years, Shigetomo was also heavily influenced by the Baiduist priest Genkai, adopting [[Jōdo-shū]] as his primary religion, which stood in stark contrast to his increasingly violent martial duties.

==Sengoku period samurai==
[[File:Honda Shigetomo B.png|250px|thumb|left|A portrait of a younger Honda Shigetomo, painted by Yota Mikatani.]]
[[File:Honda Shigetomo campaign map.svg|500px|thumb|right|Map of Honda Shigetomo's campaigns across the Okaiken archipelago (1582–1625). Numbered pins trace the chronological sequence of battles and key locations.]]
{{Campaigns of Honda Shigetomo| embed=yes}}

Shigetomo's introduction to warfare was abrupt and traumatic. In the early summer of 1593, following a breakdown in relations, Takigawa Kazumasu launched a surprise attack on the Honda stronghold of [[Suemori]]. The eleven-year-old Shigetomo narrowly escaped alongside his father as the Honda forces suffered heavy initial losses before fighting the Takigawa to a bloody stalemate. This sudden betrayal by a former ally deeply impacted Shigetomo, fostering a lifelong paranoia and a belief in overwhelming preemptive force.

Following the stalemate, Takigawa officially sanctioned the Second Invasion of [[Sanzu]] in 1596 to direct the Honda clan's military ambitions away from the capital. After Shigetada successfully subjugated the [[Uesugi clan]] through a massive spearhead assault, he was appointed Governor-General of Northern Yagikoshu and established his base in the sleepy port of [[Tsuda, Kochi Prefecture|Tsuda]]. It was here, in 1597 at the age of fifteen, that Shigetomo formally came of age. He assisted his father in transforming Tsuda into an economic and military powerhouse, observing the implementation of free market (''rakuichi rakuza'') policies and the influx of wealth from the Sado gold mines that forged the "Honda Military Machine."

Shigetomo was quickly deployed to various mop-up operations across the archipelago. He first joined his older brother and father at the Siege of Matsubara, near [[Morimoto]] in [[Kurosaki Prefecture]], where they wrested power from the Ito clan, forcing them to surrender and become subservient daimyos. He was subsequently deployed to the [[Battle of Yamanote]], fought near the city of [[Hirogawa]] on the southern coast of [[Harujima]]—a naval expedition across the strait from Yagikoshu—where Shigetomo served as a flank commander for the allied Takigawa troops. According to the ''Mitsukoji'', his unit suffered heavy casualties, but the army attained victory, and Shigetomo was highly praised for his frontline leadership, sustaining minor wounds to his right arm.

As his father's trusted vanguard, Shigetomo played a vital role in the unauthorized late 1599 invasion of [[Ishijima]]. While the Murakami Navy maintained a strict blockade, Shigetomo led ground forces against the fierce naval warriors of the [[Chosokabe clan]], participating in the decisive [[Battle of Nonichi]] near [[Ikusaka]] in [[Suzuka Prefecture]] in January 1600 that forced Chosokabe Motochika's surrender.

===The Battle of Tone and Subjugation Campaigns===
[[File:Shigetomo being tended.png|thumb|left|250px|A depiction of Shigetomo in the aftermath of the Battle of Tone]]
The tension between the Takigawa and Honda clans shattered the realm following Takigawa Kazumasu's sudden death in June 1600. During the resulting succession war, which culminated in the massive [[Battle of Tone]] on 21 October 1600—fought on the plains near [[Omiya]] in [[Mito Prefecture]], close to the ancient capital of [[Mito]]—the eighteen-year-old Shigetomo commanded the Eastern Army's vanguard reserves. When the battlefield devolved into the chaotic, fog-shrouded melee known as the "Crimson Fog" due to cascading betrayals, Shigetomo held the rear lines, preventing the total collapse of the Honda right flank. Though the victory was pyrrhic, costing the Honda forces nearly fifty percent casualties, it secured their absolute supremacy over Okaiken.

The political landscape shifted entirely on 12 January 1601, when Emperor Komei formally recognized Honda Shigetada as the lost Imperial Prince Fumihito. With this Restoration, the entire Honda clan was abruptly elevated to imperial royalty. While his older brother, Nabenosuke, seamlessly transitioned into the role of an imperial prince, Shigetomo was deeply unsettled by the revelation. Having spent his formative years as a hostage and his youth on bloody battlefields, he felt profoundly alienated by the archaic traditions and strict protocols of the court nobles in Okami.

According to the ''Mitsukoji'', Shigetomo wrote several impassioned letters to his father during this period, explicitly rejecting his newfound royal status. He argued that his hands were "stained with the mud of Tone" and that he belonged with the soldiers and commoners he had bled alongside, not behind a silk screen. He formally petitioned his father to strip him of any imperial titles so he could continue serving as the sword of the clan. Recognizing both his son's absolute loyalty and his unparalleled effectiveness as a military enforcer, Shigetada granted the request. He officially exempted Shigetomo from imperial court duties and instead granted him supreme governable authority over the pacification of the provinces. This critical decision laid the direct groundwork for the Dual Sovereignty system, allowing Shigetomo to operate as the military arm of the state while his father and brother handled the divine rites.

Acting on this new authority, Shigetomo was tasked with subduing the remaining pockets of resistance. His most famous early military achievement occurred during the twin battles of [[Battle of Nakasendo|Nakasendo]] and [[Battle of Ishikawada|Ishikawada]] in March 1602. The [[Ii clan]], led by the fierce veteran [[Ii Naomasa]], refused to submit to the new Honda Shogunate. Shigetomo spearheaded the shogunate's army, decisively defeating Naomasa at Nakasendo, near [[Iroha]] in [[Suzuka Prefecture]]. Days later at Ishikawada, near [[Doshi]] in [[Tachibara Prefecture]], Shigetomo personally defeated the forces led by his childhood friend, [[Ii Naotaka]]. Rather than annihilating the clan, Shigetomo utilized their shared history to negotiate a peaceful surrender. Through Shigetomo's direct intervention and encouragement, Naomasa became a highly trusted vassal of the Honda clan, and Shigetomo rebuilt his close relationship with Naotaka, placing him within his inner circle.

===The Ishijima Highland Campaign===
{{Main|Ishijima Highland Campaign}}
With the Ii pacified, Shigetada turned to the last pocket of organized resistance in the archipelago: the volcanic highlands of central [[Ishijima]]. The initial invasion of Ishijima in 1599–1600 had broken the [[Chōsokabe clan|Chōsokabe]] and secured the Katsuran Sea coast, but the mountainous interior remained beyond Honda control. The [[Kuroda clan]], a highland house that had survived the Ogasawara collapse by retreating into the Shimabara volcanic range, controlled the mountain passes between the western coast and the Shendan Ocean seaboard. Their lord, {{nihongo|Kuroda Mototaka|黒田元隆}}, refused all summons to submit, fortifying the volcanic ridgelines and using the crater lakes and sulfur vents as natural moats.

In the spring of 1602, Shigetada launched the Ishijima Highland Campaign, staging the invasion from the [[Seto Peninsula]]—near [[Minatojima]] in [[Seto Prefecture]]—on the northern coast of [[Yagikoshu]]—the narrowest crossing point of the Katsuran Strait. Shigetomo commanded the vanguard, [[Ii Naomasa]] led the Red Devil cavalry in support, and [[Sakakibara Yasumasa]]—the third of the Four Pillars, the shogunate's master of siege and fortification—directed the reduction of the mountain strongholds. The campaign employed the "Sword and Sutra" strategy that Shigetada had developed for contested religious territories: the Shingon Baiduist temples of the [[Tochigi Temple Route]] were offered protection and land guarantees in exchange for disarmament and open passage, while the secular holdouts—Kuroda and his vassals—were given the sword.

The volcanic highlands, however, negated the Red Devil cavalry's greatest advantage. The narrow mountain passes and loose volcanic soil made coordinated squadron charges impossible. Naomasa, frustrated by the terrain, repeatedly dismounted his cavalry and led them on foot into the Kuroda fortifications, fighting in the sulfur-choked passes with a ferocity that even the ''Mitsukoji'' describes as "unhinged." It was during one such assault—a frontal push against a Kuroda blockhouse on the northern slope of [[Aonashiyama]] ({{nihongo|青梨山|Aonashiyama|lit. "blue pear mountain"}}), a 1,371-metre volcanic peak in the central highlands of [[Ishijima]] near [[Otoge]] in [[Kiso Prefecture]]—that Naomasa was struck by a matchlock ball in the lower torso. The wound was not immediately fatal, but the bullet could not be fully extracted, and the volcanic soil that entered the wound ensured it would never heal cleanly.

The Kuroda held out for another year, fighting a grim rearguard action through the volcanic ridges. Kuroda Mototaka was finally killed in the autumn of 1603 at the [[Siege of Kuroda|fall of his mountain fortress]], choosing to charge the Honda lines alone rather than surrender. His son, Kuroda Motoaki, was captured and spared—Shigetomo's intervention, again, overruling Naomasa's demand for total extermination. The Kuroda clan was absorbed into the shogunate as a minor ''tozama'' house, relocated to a coastal domain far from their mountain stronghold.

Naomasa, characteristically, refused to withdraw from active command after his wound. He continued to ride and to lead throughout the final months of the campaign, allowing the injury to worsen through repeated exertion. By the spring of 1604, the chronic infection had taken hold. Naomasa died on May 21, 1604, in his residence in [[Okami]], aged 43. The hot season inflamed the wound that had never closed, and the fever that had plagued him intermittently became unremitting. His final words to his son [[Ii Naotaka]] were a warning against leniency—a warning born directly from the Kuroda campaign, where Shigetomo's mercy had, in Naomasa's view, left a defeated enemy alive to breed future resentment.<ref name="Mitsukoji" />

[[File:Honda Shigetomo D.png|250px|thumb|right|A depiction of Shigetomo practicing with a yari spear.]]
Shigetomo's reputation for absolute ruthlessness was cemented during the Hori clan rebellion. While Shigetomo was away conducting peace negotiations with the Amago clan in Aizawa, Hori Harunobu challenged the shogunate's authority by laying siege to Iwamura, near [[Mano]] in [[Nagato Prefecture]]. Kicked out of Aizawa upon news of the rebellion, an enraged Shigetomo returned to [[Toyozawa]] and launched a brutal counteroffensive. This culminated in the Battle of Morikawa on 18 July 1604, near [[Taniguchi]] in [[Nagato Prefecture]], where the Hori were utterly destroyed. Shigetomo ordered the execution of all surviving Hori clan members in Iwamura, taking over the domain and expanding his personal holdings by 200,000 koku.

He continued to crush minor uprisings throughout the early 1600s, peaking at the Battle of Futaba, near [[Minamiise]] in [[Kagami Prefecture]]. His merciless combat tactics and his habit of executing captured instigators on the battlefield earned him the terrifying moniker of the Punisher ({{nihongo|Oshioki|お仕置き}}). Contemporary records note that his father, Shigetada, was both impressed and unsettled by Shigetomo's bloodlust, reportedly observing him unable to sheathe his sword due to the amount of blood dried on the blade. Recognizing his son's absolute effectiveness as a military enforcer, Shigetada assigned Shigetomo to command Toyozawa, merging it with Iwamura for a massive allocation of over 580,000 koku, positioning him perfectly to succeed the military arm of the newly established Dual Sovereignty system.

===The Long Peace and the Void===
With the Kuroda destroyed, the Hori exterminated, and the last holdouts of the Sengoku period extinguished, the archipelago fell silent. The [[Battle of Tone]] had cost nearly 100,000 lives in a single day. The subsequent campaigns—the Ii subjugation, the Ishijima highlands, the Hori rebellion—had bled the warrior caste further. Shigetada, looking at the exhausted nation, saw what his generals could not: the [[Yanbaru|Dragon Kingdom]] to the north and [[Nakamizu]] to the south were watching the Sengoku bleed itself to death. If the Honda continued fighting, even in victory, the two maritime powers would invade a nation that had no soldiers left to defend itself.

The ceasefire Shigetada decreed after Tone was not leniency—it was survival. But Ii Naomasa, in the years before his death, had bitterly opposed it. He argued that any enemy left alive was a future threat, that the Honda should press the advantage while the enemy was broken. The disagreement was never formally resolved; Shigetada simply overruled him. But it left a wound in their relationship that never closed. Naomasa never openly defied Shigetada again, but he began to speak more frequently of the dangers of "generosity to wolves."

When Naomasa died on May 21, 1604, the wound became a void. He had been the bridge between the shogunate and the Ii clan—the only man who possessed both Shigetada's unshakable trust and the fierce love of his son Naotaka. Without him, the relationship between the Honda and the Ii would be conducted entirely through bureaucratic channels: edicts, land surveys, and relocation orders, each one interpreted by the Ii through Naomasa's lens of suspicion.

Shigetada's decision to appoint Shigetomo as Shogun in 1610 was, in part, a response to this void. Naomasa, once the natural military successor, had drifted from him in the years before his death. Shigetada needed a commander he could still reach—someone who saw the world as he saw it, who understood the strategic necessity of the ceasefire, who would not challenge the turn from war to governance. Shigetomo, the boy who had sat in the mud of Tone and wanted to build systems rather than wage war, was that man. The appointment was not a reward for military prowess. It was an attempt to fill the gap that Naomasa's alienation and death had created—a gap that would never truly close.

In the six years between the end of the campaigns and his formal investiture as Shogun, Shigetomo began the work that would define his reign. He studied the cadastral records of the provinces he had conquered, began drafting the legal codes that would become the [[Kinzō reforms]], and assembled the administrative triad—Kono Kageyu, Aoyama Gozaemon, and Oya Suzaemon—who would translate his vision of a nation governed by law rather than by the sword into institutional reality. The Punisher, slowly, was becoming the reformer.

==Shogun==
[[File:Toyozawa Castle.png|250px|thumb|left|Toyozawa Castle.]]

After Honda Shigetada acceded to the Imperial throne in 1610 and assumed the name Shirakawa, Shigetomo was officially invested as the second Shogun. Upon assuming the title, Shigetomo's personal land holdings were drastically reduced from his previous peak of 580,000 koku to just over 200,000 koku. This was a deliberate and highly strategic move; Shigetomo voluntarily transferred the bulk of his personal territory to the central bakufu treasury to fund national infrastructure, and distributed the remainder to key fudai daimyos to create an impenetrable buffer zone around the capital of Okami. By sacrificing his personal wealth for the stability of the state, he successfully signaled to the rest of the nation that the era of self-serving warlord land-grabbing was completely over.

During his tenure as Shogun, Shigetomo underwent a profound personal transformation. The volatile, bloodthirsty warlord once feared as the Punisher slowly matured into a calm, pragmatic, and remarkably benevolent administrator. This character shift was heavily influenced by the crushing administrative burdens of statecraft, the pacifying influence of his wife Lady Saigō, and the guidance of his religious mentor, [[Shimotsuma Rairen]]. Shigetomo began to prioritize legal codes and economic stability over martial force, intentionally surrounding himself with a cabinet of highly capable reformists rather than purely military generals. 

While Shirakawa operated from his retirement palace and guided the overarching framework of the government, Shigetomo personally appointed a triad of brilliant administrators who became the foundational pillars of his shogunate. {{nihongo|[[Kono Kageyu]]|河野勘解由}}, a brilliant legal scholar, served as the first head of the newly formed inspectorate. Kono helped draft the early martial and civil codes, effectively channeling Shigetomo's strict sense of justice into a codified legal system rather than battlefield executions. {{nihongo|[[Aoyama Gozaemon]]|青山五左衛門}}, a visionary financial architect, took over the national treasury. Aoyama was the mastermind behind the standardization of the new tax codes and would later serve as the primary administrator for the Okami Rice Exchange. Finally, {{nihongo|[[Oya Suzaemon]]|大谷須左衛門}}, an unparalleled master of civil engineering, was tasked by Shigetomo with physically connecting the fractured nation, making him the chief overseer of all national road and bridge construction projects.

In addition to this core triad, his cabinet included Sessai Taigen, a Nichiren monk who possessed extensive knowledge of foreign affairs and formulated the world's oldest continuing trade agreement between YYY and Okaiken, and Shimotsuma Rairen, a Jōdo-shū monk and Shigetomo's lifelong friend, who served as the spiritual and religious adviser to the shogunate, continually helping to temper Shigetomo's lingering Sengoku-era paranoia.

===Arrival of Lacuña===
{{main|Ernesto Lacuña|Okaiken-Dagit Relations}}
[[File:Honda Shigetomo A.png|250px|thumb|right|A painting of a Honda Shigetomo with his signature sleeveless armor.]]

On October 31, 1615, a [[Volisania]]n galleon, the ''Matricia'', carrying the outgoing Governor-General of [[Dagit]], [[Ernesto Lacuña]], was found grounded on the treacherous coast of Tosaki, a village in [[Yamatohama]], [[Iwate Prefecture]], approximately 56 kilometres northeast of [[Okami]]. Initially receiving reports of a heavily armed foreign vessel breaching Honda territorial waters, Shigetomo reacted with his trademark aggression. Anticipating a colonial incursion, he personally mobilized a vanguard of 1,200 elite cavalry and arquebusiers, executing a lightning-fast forced march to Tosaki. Upon arrival, Shigetomo orchestrated a flawless tactical encirclement of the beachhead, demonstrating the terrifying martial prowess of the Honda military machine and leaving the shipwrecked survivors entirely at his mercy.

[[File:Shigetomo Lacuna meeting for the first time.png|thumb|left|300px|A depiction of Shigetomo's first meeting with Ernesto Lacuña.]]
However, as the fully armored Shigetomo rode down to the surf, ready to order a mass execution, he was met by Lacuña. The Governor-General, despite being drenched and exhausted, stepped forward and offered his own life in exchange for the safety of his 175 crew members. This moment is widely cited by historians as a definitive turning point in Shigetomo's reign. Abandoning the persona of the Punisher, Shigetomo was deeply moved by Lacuña's honorable display. He ordered his arquebusiers to lower their matchlocks. While he formally declared the wrecked galleon and its cargo as seized property of the state, he immediately shifted to a diplomatic approach.

Shigetomo escorted the survivors back to Okami not as prisoners of war, but as unexpected guests. He provided them with extensive medical care, warm clothing, and personally hosted a lavish feast of poultry and wild game—an extraordinary gesture of goodwill. During their stay in the capital, which lasted until November 25, Shigetomo and Lacuña engaged in extensive geopolitical discussions. 

[[File:Shigetomo Lacuna gathering.png|thumb|right|300px|A depiction of Shigetomo and Lacuña's dinner reception at Okami Castle.]]
Lacuña proved instrumental in bridging the cultural divide. Deeply grateful for Shigetomo's clemency, the Governor-General leveraged his influence to solidify a lasting, peaceful relationship between Mitoyagi, the colonial administration in Dagit, and the broader Volisanian Empire. Lacuña's diplomatic maneuvering convinced the Volisanian crown to recognize the Honda Shogunate as a sovereign equal rather than a target for colonization. This vital treaty prevented a devastating colonial war and laid the groundwork for the lucrative, state-controlled trade networks Shigetomo would soon establish.

In Lacuña's published diary,<ref name="Lacuña">Lacuña, Ernesto. (1628) ''Diario de un náufrago: Mis viajes a Dagit y las islas Sanese''. Volisanian Royal Press.</ref> he described the encounters with Shigetomo as profoundly mutual and respectful. He famously recorded an instance where Shigetomo, shedding the rigid pride of a Sengoku warlord, ceremonially kissed Lacuña's foot as a sign of supreme diplomatic respect for the foreign dignitary, while Lacuña deeply bowed his head in return.

===Government reforms===
{{main|Kinzō reforms}}

In the summer of 1623, Honda Shigetomo announced sweeping changes to the ''kokudaka'' system, a distribution system of land using surveys done by the lords. During the Sengoku period, the ''kokudaka'' system was the primary reason why regional and local conflicts kept on escalating; through consent given by the imperial family, each powerful clan would be given territory, sometimes overlapping and overriding the owner of the land and causing more conflict. Honda Shigetomo enacted a decree called the Kinzō reforms which allowed independent surveyors to take over the surveying of land. This is the first time in Sanese History that a system was nationalized by the government. It would not happen again until 1871, when the [[Constitution of Okaiken]] nationalized several organizations.<ref name="Kinzō">''The Letters and Edicts of the Kinzō Reforms'' (1623-1625). Heianji Temple Archives. Translated and published by the University of Okami Press, 2016.</ref>

The decree also allowed the shogunate to manipulate control of each clans and assigned them to another territory, effectively weakening the challenge to the Honda clan. Notable warlords such as Ogasawara Kagemasa and Mukai Takanao were moved further from the shogunate control in Okami to the outlying islands like Yagikoshu and Shimizu in exchange for rice stipends. These were known as the tozama daimyos. Daimyos that remain loyal to the clan were given territories that are small but very important in safekeeping hostile people out the shogunate's control. These are called the fudai daimyos.

===Economic and Infrastructural Projects===
Beyond the cadastral surveys of the Kinzō reforms, Shigetomo enacted two other major projects to centralize and modernize Okaiken. Recognizing that the kokudaka system made the entire economy dependent on the fluctuating price of rice, he established the Okami Rice Exchange ({{nihongo|Okami Kome-shō|大上米商}}) in 1618. This institution allowed daimyō and merchants to trade in rice futures—contracts for rice to be delivered at a later date. This stabilized the price of rice, reduced the economic shock of bad harvests, and provided the shogunate with greater financial control. This exchange was the direct precursor to the modern Sankei 325 Index.<ref name="Kinzō" />

Concurrently, Shigetomo ordered the construction of the Hokuto Omi - Shinshu road. This massive infrastructural project, completed in 1622, was the first major engineered highway to traverse the entire length of Harujima island. It cut travel times from the northern provinces to the capital from weeks to days, enabling the rapid movement of troops, tax levies, and commercial goods. The road connected the previously isolated northern han directly to the shogunate's center of power in Okami, further weakening the autonomy of potential tozama lords.

==Personal life and family==
Shigetomo was married to  {{nihongo|Lady Saigō|西郷の方|Saigō-no-kata|}}, a daughter of the allied Saigō clan, in 1603 to cement the Honda's alliance with them during the unification campaigns. She was known as a patron of the arts and a devout Jōdō-shu Baiduist, reportedly having a calming influence on Shigetomo's often volatile temperament.

He had four children listed in the official genealogies: his eldest son and successor, Honda Shigemasa (1605–1651); his only daughter, Chiyo (1607–1629), who was married to Okudaira Tadamasa to secure the shogunate's inner circle of allies; a second son, Honda Tadamasa (1608–1633), who was later adopted by Shigetomo's brother, Shigetoki, to ensure the continuation of that branch family; and an adopted son, Honda Shigeie (1611–1640), who was, according to the ''Mitsukoji'', the son of loyal retainer Hotta Muneshige, adopted by Shigetomo to bind the Hotta clan permanently to the main Honda line.<ref name="Mitsukoji" />

Contemporaneous accounts describe Shigetomo as a man of contradictions. He possessed the "outside-the-box wisdom" noted in his youth and was a keen reformer, yet he was also known for his "Punisher" nickname and a fierce temper. After the Kinzō Incidents and the death of his father, this volatility reportedly worsened, leading to the bouts of depression, heavy drinking, and erratic behavior documented in the lead-up to the Battle of Ichijoudani.<ref name="Mitsukoji" />

==Ichijoudani and Death==
{{Main|Battle of Ichijoudani}}
Honda Shigetomo ({{nihongo|重朝|Shigetomo}}), also known as the Punisher in his early years and later deified as the God of Seals ({{nihongo|封印の神|Fūin no Kami}}), died on 18 June 1625 during the [[Siege of Ichijoudani]], aged 42. His death—occurring in under 135 seconds after being struck by a tanegashima round—is one of the most studied and mythologized moments in Okaiken history, marking the end of the Kinzō Incidents and the beginning of the [[Great Reconciliation]].

===Outset and the Siege===
[[File:Shigetomo Drinking.png|thumb|left|250px|Shigetomo consuming alcohol after frantically arriving at Toyozawa.]]
After the escalation of the Kinzō Incidents that saw the death of his father, Emperor Shirakawa, Shigetomo began to act erratically, according to the ''[[Mitsukoji]]''. He visited the remains of the Toshoji temple in Toyozawa on 8 June to see what had happened at the temple after the incident. Ordering all of his soldiers to leave him alone while visiting the ruins, he lost his temperament and vowed to seek revenge at all costs. The account continued to describe his deteriorating behavior; by the night of 9 June, he was said to be consuming alcohol in heavy quantities.

Shigetomo began calling the nearby units of the Teruzumi, Okudaira, Manabe, and [[Hotta clan]]s, which remained loyal to the shogunate, to invade the Ii territory at Ichijoudani beginning 12 June. Historical anecdotes indicate that his erratic behavior severely affected the effectiveness of the assembled army. Several clans also took advantage of the weakened state of the Honda clan's control over the country. Second-in-command [[Hotta Muneshige]] took control of the advancing units of the Hotta and the Okudaira clans and deployed the company at Minatokoriyama, near [[Ise-Takahama]] in [[Ise Prefecture]], about 8 kilometres northeast of Ichijoudani castle. Shigetomo remained at the main base at the foot of Mount Hei, near [[Eiheiji]] in [[Ise Prefecture]], in the shadow of [[Kiriinutsugemine]] ({{nihongo|霧犬槇峰|Kiriinutsugemine|lit. "fog dog cedar peak"}}), a 1,595-metre peak that dominates the Ichijoudani valley.

By 11 June, Hotta's secondary forces participated in the Battle of Domyōji—near [[Takanashi]] in [[Ise Prefecture]]—against the forces of [[Kimura Shigetora]], which had pledged against the shogunate after the Kinzō incident. The secondary force was successful in routing the Kimura forces out of Domyōji and began to advance closer to Ichijoudani castle by the morning of 12 June. Shigetomo had also begun his advance to the castle, winning minor skirmishes against Muzen Moritaka and Inaba Shigemasa. 

By 13 June, the main and the secondary forces recombined at the fork of the Ichijoudani and Akagawa Rivers, preparing for an assault on the castle. From the ''Mitsukoji'', it is said that during the recombination of the troops on 16 June, Shigetomo continued consuming heavy amounts of alcohol and injured Hotta Muneshige after a physical altercation. This would later reflect on Muneshige's decision to withdraw his forces at the request of the Okudaira army, as he saw that Shigetomo was not fit to lead the army to the siege, leaving the Honda clan to set up an encampment in front of Ichijoudani castle on 17 June.

On 17 June, the Ii troops launched a full frontal assault against the Honda forces. Notable Honda retainers such as Manabe Hachisuke and Kawada Muneharu were killed during the early stages of the assault, led on two fronts by Ii Naotaka and Kimura Shigetora. However, in a major blow to the Ii forces, Kimura Shigetora was killed during the battle against Honda retainers Ichizen Yasushige and Watanabe Tatsuyasu, along with 675 other casualties. The Ii clan, which had already penetrated the main Honda forces, were forced to retreat and return to Ichijoudani Castle. 

===The accepted historical narrative===
[[File:Shigetomo Ichijoudani.png|thumb|right|250px|A depiction of Shigetomo at Ichijoudani.]]
For centuries, scholars debated the motives of why Shigetomo approached the camp alone during the early morning hours of 18 June. In the accepted historical event, Shigetomo had asked his younger brother, [[Honda Shigetoki]], to move his soldiers 25 miles north of the castle to open a new front against the Ii clan's positions in Ichijoudani. Shigetomo then asked Hotta Muneshige and [[Sessai Taigen]] to advance towards the castle at around dawn to divert the Ii clan's attention from Shigetoki's forces north. 

According to this traditional account, Shigetomo approached the Ii encampment at the banks of the Musashino River, in the [[Ichijoudani]] valley of [[Ise Prefecture]] for mysterious reasons, either to negotiate defections from the Ii clan to the imperial forces or to provoke the Ii clan into abandoning their lord in exchange for Ichijoudani. The retainers of the Ii clan refused, stating that their lives would not be spared due to their involvement with the assassination of the Emperor.

With the offer refused, Shigetomo was said to be agitated and charged towards the castle walls in frustration. It was long recorded that he was accidentally shot by a foot soldier guarding the walls of the fortification, with the shot hitting Shigetomo on his upper right chest and the bullet embedding in his upper right lung, causing it to bleed profusely. Shigetomo reportedly fell from his horse and aggravated his injuries.

The older narrative claimed that Ii Naotaka along with his retainers approached Shigetomo, who was bleeding to death. One of Naotaka's senior retainers, Saitō Toshitatsu, had Shigetomo subdued to the castle wall and was about to pierce Shigetomo with a yari when Naotaka intervened and said that the incident is only between Shigetomo and himself. After Shigetomo apologized to Naotaka for not telling him in advance about the move, he requested to commit seppuku with Naotaka taking his head after. Unable to commit seppuku as he had significantly weakened, Shigetomo asked Naotaka to outright behead him. Naotaka beheaded Shigetomo in accordance with his last wishes and displayed his head on the castle gate. Saitō Toshitatsu later handed over the letter he found on Shigetomo's body to Naotaka. According to this myth, Ii Naotaka would himself later perish when a cannonball landed on the castle's Ni-no-maru compound and hit his back.

===Forensic sequence of death===
[[File:Shigetomo shot.png|thumb|right|250px|A depiction of Shigetomo getting shot.]]
However, modern scholars have since overturned much of this accepted history. The exact physical sequence of Shigetomo's death has been meticulously reconstructed using battlefield artifacts, declassified texts, and modern forensic analysis. On the early morning hours of 18 June, Shigetomo actually dismounted his horse approximately twenty meters from the main gate of Ichijoudani Castle before any shots were fired. As he stood alone in the mud, a 15-millimeter lead ball fired from an Ii matchlock struck him. Contrary to the traditional belief of an upper chest wound, forensic analysis of his preserved armor indicates an oblique entry into the lower left torso, penetrating the external oblique muscle and critically lacerating the liver and branches of the portal vein. The impact jolted Shigetomo backward; he immediately dropped his truce flag and clamped his right hand over the entry wound as blood rapidly soaked through his white jinbaori and cracked black dō plate.

Within twenty seconds of the impact, Shigetomo collapsed. His knees buckled, and he pitched forward into the mud, his face turning so that his right cheek rested on the ground. Recognizing the catastrophic error, Ii Naotaka reportedly sprinted from the gate to the fallen shogun. Naotaka rotated Shigetomo's body from a prone to a supine position, cradling his childhood friend's head and shoulders in his lap while frantically attempting to wipe the blood from Shigetomo's nose and mouth. 

[[File:Shigetomo dying.png|thumb|left|250px|A depiction of Shigetomo dying in the mud outside the castle gates.]]
Shortly thereafter, Hotta Muneshige arrived from the Honda encampment and knelt beside them, securing Shigetomo's left hand across his chest plate. It was at this moment that Naotaka reached inside Shigetomo's armor and retrieved two folded, bloodstained letters, reading them aloud. Shigetomo, experiencing rapid hypovolemic shock, was largely unresponsive, managing only faint, wet rasps. Approximately one minute and forty seconds after the shot, Shigetomo entered the agonal phase, characterized by a weak, rattling breath and a brief upward shrug of his shoulders. His breathing ceased entirely at the two-minute mark, and clinical death followed moments later as his body went completely limp.

===Events from the ''Mitsukoji''===
{{multiple image
| align     = right
| direction = vertical
| image1    = Damaged armor of Shigetomo.png
| width     = 300
| image2    = Honda Shigetomo Shitagi.jpg
| footer    = Honda Shigetomo's damaged armor (top) and his bloodstained shitagi (undershirt, bottom). Both are now displayed along with Ii Naotaka's armor at the National Museum of History in Okami. While the armor displays damage to the upper left section, it was later determined that it was a transportation damage. The actual, smaller hole in the armor, where the bullet entered, was discovered in 2025, indicating a clean entry.
}}
While modern forensics provide the clinical timeline, the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'' chronicle dramatizes the immense emotional weight of the tragedy and corrects the myths surrounding Naotaka's death. According to the official shogunate record, on the early morning hours of the 18th, against his retainers' wishes, Shigetomo approached Ichijoudani castle by himself to pursue Naotaka to the gates of the castle. Once he reached the castle gate, Shigetomo, who was in a state of rage and agitation, challenged Naotaka to a sword duel. With reluctance, Naotaka declined to duel him because of their longstanding relationship as well as for the safety of both his and Shigetomo's life. Shigetomo, in an increasingly erratic behavior, began to point his spear toward the gate, threatening to kill Naotaka and the rest of the Ii clan. At the same time, Shigetomo began to shed tears and was about to return to the camp when one of the Ii ashigaru then fired shots, despite Naotaka's orders to not shoot Shigetomo at all cost. 

Hit in the lower torso, Shigetomo managed to remain conscious momentarily, screaming in agony before collapsing just 10 metres from the main gate of the castle. Blood then began to flow from the bullet wound and from his mouth and nose, generating a pool of blood. Naotaka abandoned his post, sprinting down the stone steps to personally unbar the heavy wooden doors and surge out into the mud. Simultaneously, hearing the gunshot, Honda retainers Hotta Muneshige, Ito Shinhei, and the monk Sessai Taigen burst from the thick fog on horseback. Vaulting from his saddle, Muneshige reached the fallen shogun just as Naotaka did. Believing Naotaka had orchestrated the assassination, Muneshige lunged in a blind fury, slashing a deep gash across Naotaka's left arm.

The two sides clashed directly over the dying Shogun until Shigetomo weakly raised his bloodied left hand, a gesture carrying such absolute authority that the fighting instantly ceased. With agonizing slowness, Shigetomo reached inside his breastplate and pulled out a set of folded, soaked papers. As he pressed the letters toward Naotaka, the chronicle notes a highly emotional exchange, stating that Shigetomo shed a single tear and gasped fragmented apologies to both his childhood friend and his loyal retainer:

{{quote|Manchiyo, Yachiman... I am sorry. It was all my fault. My irrational actions had led to the destruction of everything that I loved. I am going to die in vain for nothing. I should not have done it. Manchiyo, Yachiman, don't let me die in vain. Yachi, I beg you... forgive Manchiyo. Manchiyo... I am truly sorry.|excerpt from the ''Mitsukoji'', page 774<ref name="Mitsukoji" />}}

As severe hemorrhagic shock set in and his breathing devolved into rapid, shallow clicks, Shigetomo partially closed his eyes and delivered his famous final directive, establishing the philosophical foundation for the peace that followed:

{{quote|{{nihongo|The true enemy was my very own existence. Let everyone come to my grave and rethink of their existence and seal it.|戒むべきは実在なり、我が墓に詣でる者をもう必ず意味実在を封じて考えろっじゃ}}|excerpt from the ''Mitsukoji'', page 774<ref name="Mitsukoji" />}}

The chronicle concludes the tragedy with a profound act of shared sacrifice. Shigetomo lost consciousness and died shortly after his final words. Yoritsuna Kagemasa, the retainer who ordered the initial assault, committed seppuku on the basis of dishonoring both sides. In the immediate aftermath, honoring Shigetomo's pre-written request to be spared an agonizing, prolonged death, Naotaka severed his head with a clean horizontal cut. Overwhelmed by grief, Naotaka then unfolded the blood-soaked letters Shigetomo had handed him. As his eyes scanned the ruined calligraphy, he realized it was a formal decree of forgiveness, alongside a drafted guarantee securing Naotaka's transfer to a one-million-koku domain in Higo. Shigetomo had come to the gates not to kill him, but to mend their brotherhood.

Realizing the war was a tragic misunderstanding, Naotaka offered his own head to Hotta Muneshige for the treason that had transpired. Muneshige, his face carved with grief, declined, stating the Shogun had asked him to forgive Naotaka and allow him the honor of a samurai's death. Brandishing his sword, Naotaka committed seppuku beside Shigetomo in the mud of the castle courtyard, assisted by Muneshige, who acted as his second. 

Shigetomo and Naotaka are buried opposing each other, in Ichijoudani; both were 42 years old. Shigetomo was given the name {{nihongo|Sankō-in Tonomaegumo-shū Kishiyo Ryōgen Daikoji|三光院殿前雲州岸譽良玄大居士}} after his death. Bone fragments were also sent to his domain temple at Ryōgen-ji in Okami and at the family shrine in [[Mitsui]]. The spot where he died is now marked with a memorial, along with a statue.

==Aftermath==
[[File:Ryogenji Shamoji.jpg|250px|thumb|left|''Shamoji'' at Shigetomo's grave, Ichijoudani.]]
In the aftermath of the battle and the deaths of Shigetomo and Naotaka, the shogunate gave the Ii clan and other clans that were allied with clemency. The Kinzō reforms were done slowly but was continued and reassured by the new shogun Shigetoki, Shigetomo's younger brother. The Ii clan kept their territory in Ichijoudani, but was stripped of more than 70,000 ''koku'' for reparations to the death of Shigetomo and Shigetada. The Ii clan agreed to pay the reparations and promised to help the Honda shogunate with government. Eventually the Ii became a dominant figure in Sanese politics during the Imperial shogunate period.

After the death of Tairo [[Ii Naoyoshi]] in 1864, the Ii family vacated their territory in Ichijoudani and were demoted to the territory of Kozuka, a 10,000 ''koku'' territory but nonetheless important in Sanese trade during the late Honda period. When the shogunate was dissolved and the ''han'' system abolished, both the Ii and the Honda clans were absorbed into the peerage system. The Honda clan remained influential in the 20th and 21st Century politics, with [[Honda Tadashige]], [[Honda Shigehara]] and
[[Honda Shigetsuma]] elected [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|Prime Ministers]] in 1961, 1991 and 2020, respectively. The Ii clan remained in Kozuka and became influential in establishing Ii Holdings Company in 1955. Ii Holdings later merged with Onkyo to form Ikyo, One of the world's leading technology companies.

===Ichijoudani today===
{{main|Ichijoudani}}
[[File:Ichijoudani Machiyas.jpg|250px|thumb|right|The main town of Ichijoudani in 2014]]

Ichijoudani was rebuilt after the battle, but most of the services were shifted away from the battle site. What was once the town's central area was replaced by a temple, Shingo-ji; and commerce was moved further north after the battle to honour those who were killed. Shigetomo and Naotaka are buried to the opposite sides of the Heianji temple; just across from the Shingo-ji temple, with one grave looking after one another. On each anniversary of Shigetomo's and Naotaka's death, now coinciding with the town's ''Kanto'' festival, both graves were connected by a hemp rope that symbolizes their friendship. A person wearing black clothing representing as the 'demon' then cuts both ropes, which then lead to the opening of a gate that leads to the spot in the temple courtyard where Shigetomo and Naotaka died. Shigetomo is worshiped as the "God of Seals"; thousands of messages praying for an end of their struggles were written on a small paddle; it is then sealed with a red ink stamp before being placed as an offering to the temple, which is then burned to ashes at the annual festival. Naotaka is worshiped as the "God of Vindication"; thousands of messages praying for hope of the future are written in the similar paddle but it is sealed with a blank stamp. These are thrown in the Ichijoudani River after 7 days, where it flows to the Sea of Mitoyagi.

Ichijoudani remained an important centre of development in the Imperial shogunate period; when the [[Mitoyagi Empire]] was established in 1871, it became the capital of Ichijoudani Prefecture. Later, when the Ichijoudani Prefecture was later divided into Ise and Mie Prefectures, Ichijoudani lost its status as the capital of both prefectures. Even after losing its status as a prefectural capital, it grew to an important economic centre and a transport hub to Ise from Toyozawa. In 1960, when the Toyozawa - [[Asahi]] High Speed line opened, a station was built over part of the battle site and commerce was eventually allowed to be built on it due to the lack of space associated with the city.

===Release of the Honda letters===
[[File:Honda Letters Museum.jpg|thumb|left|280px|The two displayed letters written by Honda Shigetomo for Ii Naotaka]]
On April 8, 2016, the Heianji temple, in association with the University of Okami and the Toho Shuheisha University, released the so-called "Honda letters", a collection of correspondence that Honda Shigetomo wrote from the 18th of May to 17 June 1625. This archive notably included the badly damaged and bloodstained letter to Ii Naotaka that he carried when he was mortally wounded; the collection is now on display alongside various paraphernalia of the Honda and Ii clans at the National Museum of History in Okami. The letters were released to the public in three chronological stages, providing unprecedented insight into Shigetomo's psychological deterioration and the frantic diplomatic back-and-forth that preceded the shogunate's greatest crisis.

The first stage of documents, covering events from 18 May to 5 June 1625, highlights the bureaucratic frenzy of the Kinzō reforms and the escalating tensions leading up to the Kinzō Incident. During this period, Shigetomo's letters reveal a ruler obsessed with micromanagement, dispatching dozens of missives daily to various fudai daimyos demanding immediate compliance with the new land surveys. On 28 May, he sent Ii Naotaka a formal inquiry asking if everything was in order regarding his clan's impending transfer. Naotaka responded respectfully on 30 May, assuring the Shogun that the independent survey results were completed and the Ii clan was preparing for their relocation to the new domain at Higo. However, other intercepted letters from this period show Shigetomo writing to his brother, Emperor Daigo, expressing deep paranoia that the outer clans were secretly amassing weapons to resist the edicts.

The second stage of releases, spanning from 6 June to 15 June, captures the immediate, chaotic fallout of the assassination of Retired Emperor Shirakawa. The correspondence from these nine days paints a vivid picture of Shigetomo's erratic behavior and the collapse of the central command structure. Following the attack on Toyozawa, Shigetomo's calligraphy becomes noticeably rushed, with frequent ink splatters and crossed-out sentences. He engaged in a frantic, contradictory exchange of orders with his loyalist commanders. On 9 June, he sent orders to the Okudaira and Hotta clans to immediately raze any village harboring Ii sympathizers, only to countermand the order hours later in a subsequent letter. The most revealing documents from this stage are his direct correspondences with the Ii clan. Upon receiving a letter from Naotaka on 6 June stating he was en route to Higo through Toyozawa, Shigetomo replied on 8 June with a deeply emotional, almost incoherent missive. He oscillated wildly between threatening the total annihilation of the Ii bloodline and desperately pleading with Naotaka to deny his involvement in the Retired Emperor's death, showcasing a man torn between his duties as Shogun and his grief as a son.

The third set of letters, which covers the final 48 hours of 16 June to 17 June, was unveiled on 14 April 2016. This collection encompasses Shigetomo's final directives and several unsent drafts. Written from the main encampment at Heizan (Mount Hei), near [[Eiheiji]] in [[Ise Prefecture]], these documents demonstrate a fatalistic acceptance of his impending death. Recognizing his own compromised mental state, Shigetomo penned a formal decree to his younger brother, Honda Shigetoki, instructing him to assume the title of shogun should Shigetomo fall in battle or become incapacitated. Simultaneously, he sent a sorrowful farewell to Emperor Daigo, enclosing a finalized version of the proposed property transfers for the remaining castle lords, and lamenting his failure to maintain the peace their father had built.

The centerpiece of the museum exhibition features the two final documents known simply as the "Ii Letters", both addressed directly to Ii Naotaka. The first letter, found crumpled and unsent within the Heianji temple archives, is wildly aggressive; in it, Shigetomo demands that Naotaka surrender his head immediately or face dire, torturous consequences. The second version, however, is a complete reversal in tone. This was the letter Shigetomo carried onto the battlefield on the day of his death. In it, Shigetomo addressed Naotaka with profound vulnerability, stating that he had forgiven his childhood friend's actions and was prepared to do everything in his power to win back his friendship. It also contained a formal reassurance that the Ii domain in Higo, valued at one million koku, would be honored. This final, bloodstained letter suffered severe elemental damage and took the university research team almost two years to fully decipher, standing today as the ultimate testament to the tragic Great Reconciliation.

==Legacy==
[[File:Okami Shigetomo Statue.png|thumb|right|280px|Statue of Honda Shigetomo in [[Okami]].]]

Honda Shigetomo's legacy is one of the most hotly debated subjects in Sanese historiography. For centuries during the ''Pax Honda'', the shogunate carefully curated his image as a tragic, self-sacrificing visionary who willingly gave his life to end the cycle of Sengoku violence. This romanticized view was physically enshrined in modern Okami with the erection of a massive bronze statue of Shigetomo in [[Tama Park]]. The monument depicts the Shogun standing resolutely with his signature yari spear, wearing the sleeveless armor style inherited from his grandfather, [[Anjō Motonobu]]. It serves as a popular landmark and a physical reminder of the heavy burdens of early Sanese leadership.

However, modern scholars from the University of Okami and the Toho Shuheisha University have fiercely debated Shigetomo's true intentions during the final hours of the [[Siege of Ichijoudani]], particularly following the declassification of the ''Mitsukoji'' and the Honda letters. The central academic dispute revolves around whether Shigetomo's death was a calculated martyrdom or the chaotic result of an alcohol-fueled breakdown.

The Martyrdom Thesis, championed by traditionalist historians, argues that Shigetomo, overwhelmed by the guilt of his father's death and recognizing that his own tyrannical Kinzō reforms had pushed the nation to the brink, deliberately sought a peaceful resolution. Proponents of this view point to the second Ii Letter, carried on his person, as definitive proof that he approached the gates fully intending to forgive Naotaka and offer his own life as a political pressure release valve to save the shogunate.

Conversely, the Provocation Thesis, largely driven by revisionist scholars analyzing the erratic first drafts of his letters, suggests a much darker reality. These historians argue that Shigetomo marched to the gates in a genuine, homicidal rage, intending to force Naotaka into a duel or execute him on the spot. Under this interpretation, the forgiving letter was merely a discarded alternative drafted during a fleeting moment of sobriety, and Shigetomo's death was essentially a tragedy brought on by severe psychological collapse and the fatal misjudgment of an anxious foot soldier.

Regardless of his true internal state on that morning, historians unanimously agree that the consequences of his death were foundational to the modern Sanese state. By dying alongside Ii Naotaka, Shigetomo inadvertently created the political capital necessary for his brother, Honda Shigetoki, to enact the Great Reconciliation, successfully preserving the unified nation they had fought so violently to build.

==Mythological significance==
{{Main|Mythology of Okaiken}}

[[File:Honda Shigetomo Stone Shogun.png|thumb|left|300px|Honda Shigetomo, the {{nihongo|Iwa-no-Shogun|岩将軍|Stone Shogun}}, whose birthmark shaped like the character for "stone" (石) marked him as the human agent of Kuninushi's perpetual pounding]]

===The Iwa-no-Shogun===

Within the mythological tradition of [[Okaiken]], Honda Shigetomo occupies a central position as the human embodiment of the [[Mythology of Okaiken#The Stone Pounding Cycle|Stone Pounding Cycle]]. Where his father, Emperor Shirakawa, represented the mythological reunion of the Solar Cycle and the Stone Pounding Cycle — the sun hidden in the stone, the divine sovereign forged on the anvil of the [[Sengoku period]] — Shigetomo represents the stone alone: the pure, uncompromising force of the compact, the discipline of the forge, the authority of the anvil.

The later tradition attributes to Shigetomo the epithet {{nihongo|Iwa-no-Shogun|岩将軍|Stone Shogun}}, a title that connects him directly to [[Kuninushi]]'s sacred stone. According to the legend preserved in the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'' (1680), Shigetomo was born with a birthmark on his right palm shaped like the character for "stone" (石). The midwife who attended his birth is said to have remarked that the infant's grip was so strong that it hurt to be held — as though the boy were already gripping the sacred stone. The birthmark and the grip were taken as signs that Shigetomo had been chosen by the stone — that he was the human agent through whom Kuninushi's perpetual pounding would continue.<ref name="Mitsukoji" />

This mythological identification shaped the way the Shogunate's administrative philosophy was understood. The ''kokudaka'' system, the meritocratic bureaucracy, the emphasis on physical discipline and institutional hardening — all of these were understood by the Shogunate's proponents as the stone's governance: the systematic application of pressure to the soft silt of human society, compressing it into the hard structure of a functional state. The [[Honda Shogunate]]'s 260-year rule was, in mythological terms, the longest sustained Pounding in the archipelago's history — a period of continuous compression that produced the institutional infrastructure of modern Okaiken.

===The Niō and the Dual Sovereignty===

[[File:Nio Guardians Dual Sovereignty.png|thumb|right|300px|The Niō guardians of the Dual Sovereignty: Agyō (left, open-mouthed, identified with the Shogun as the active Pounding) and Ungyō (right, closed-mouthed, identified with the Emperor as the still Release)]]

The most consequential mythological synthesis associated with Shigetomo is the identification of the {{nihongo|Niō|仁王|Benevolent Kings}} — the two giant guardian figures who stand at the gates of [[Shoto]] shrines — with the Dual Sovereignty ({{nihongo|Ryōkai|両界}}). In this framework, the two guardian kings were mapped onto the two poles of the post-[[Battle of Tone|Tone]] constitutional order:

* {{nihongo|Agyō|阿形|Open-Mouthed King}} — active, the Pounding — was identified with the Shogun: the military governor who exercised active, compressive force on the body politic, driving out corruption and maintaining the compact through the application of disciplined pressure.
* {{nihongo|Ungyō|吽形|Closed-Mouthed King}} — still, the Release — was identified with the Emperor: the sacred sovereign who held the stillness at the center of the state, embodying the continuity of the imperial line and the spiritual authority that gave the Pounding its meaning.

This identification was not merely decorative; it was constitutional. The Edict of the Two Realms (1610), which formally established the Dual Sovereignty system under which Shigetomo operated, explicitly invokes the Niō as the mythological precedent for the division of sovereignty, stating that "as the two Kings stand at the gate of the shrine, one breathing out and one breathing in, so shall the two Sovereignties stand at the gate of the nation, one acting and one preserving." The language of the Edict — the only surviving document that formally establishes the Dual Sovereignty — is framed entirely in the mythological vocabulary of the [[Tesshin-shinkō|Iron Cycle]].

===The God of Seals===

Shigetomo's deification as the {{nihongo|Fūin no Kami|封印の神|God of Seals}} after his death at Ichijoudani represents the mythological culmination of the Stone Pounding Cycle's central metaphor. The "seal" in this context is not merely a stamp or a signet; it is the act of compression itself — the final, definitive Pounding that locks the structure of the state into place and prevents the silt from reverting to its original softness. By dying at the gates of Ichijoudani — by literally pounding his own body against the stone walls of the castle — Shigetomo was understood as completing the final blow of the compact, sealing the [[Great Reconciliation]] with his own blood.

The ritual practices associated with Shigetomo's grave at Ichijoudani reflect this mythological identification. The ''[[shamoji]]'' (rice paddles) on which visitors write their prayers are sealed with a red ink stamp before being burned — the red seal representing Shigetomo's final act of compression, the sealing of the compact between the people and the land. The practice echoes the [[inoko]] ceremony, in which children strike the ground with sacred stones to renew the compact, but at Shigetomo's grave the Pounding has been transmuted into the Seal — the blow that does not need to be struck again, because it was struck once and for all by the Stone Shogun himself.

===The Tetsu-kage===

In modern mythological discourse, Shigetomo's legacy has been reexamined through the concept of the {{nihongo|tetsu-kage|鉄蔭|iron shadow}}, coined by the political philosopher [[Ren Ishikawa]] in 2017. The tetsu-kage is the institutional shadow cast by the [[Tesshin-shinkō|Cult of Iron]] — the apparent solidity of a framework that recognizes the need for continuous Pounding while concealing the soft silt of unresolved social problems beneath its hardened surface. In this reading, Shigetomo's Shogunate established the pattern of institutional hardening that defined Okaiken's governance for centuries: the compression of social conflict into formal structures that appeared permanent but were, like all compacted silt, subject to the eventual Loosening.

The tetsu-kage concept has been applied to the [[Kinzō reforms]] specifically, with revisionist scholars arguing that Shigetomo's cadastral surveys — far from being the rational administrative measures described in traditional historiography — were the mythological act of Pounding raised to the level of state policy: the attempt to compress the soft, overlapping, contradictory land claims of the Sengoku era into the hard, uniform grid of a nationalized system. The reforms' failure and the resulting Kinzō Incidents are thus read as the mythological Loosening — the moment when the compressed silt of political tension broke through the crust of institutional force.

==In popular culture==
Shigetomo appears frequently within fiction and continues to be portrayed in many other anime, manga, video games, and cinematic films. Many depictions show him as quiet, happy and passive in nature although some portray him as a dangerous manipulator. The latter type of works include Ryuichi Otani's film ''Kagerou'', which portrays Shigetomo as explosive, dangerous and reckless. The film ''Rairen'' portrays him as a murderous lord.

By contrast, the novel and anime series ''Yōtōden'' portrays Shigetomo as a benevolent person in addition to a passive, willing-to-compromise warlord. He is portrayed as patient and passive in some anime and manga series. Shigetomo is portrayed as a mentor, concerned and full of empathy in the film ''Yuma'' by Inari Marugata.

The dichotomy in Shigetomo's popular portrayals reflects the dual nature of his mythological identity. Works that emphasize his early career as the Punisher tend to draw on the violent imagery of the [[Mythology of Okaiken#The Stone Pounding Cycle|Stone Pounding Cycle]] — the raw, uncompromising force of the compact, the blade that cannot be sheathed for the blood on it. Works that portray him as a benevolent or contemplative figure tend to draw on his later deification as the God of Seals — the final, definitive compression that brings peace through sacrifice. The 2014 [[Taisen drama]] ''Honda Tenno'' is notable for attempting to portray both aspects, tracing his arc from the bloodthirsty warlord to the tragic peacemaker who dies at the gates of Ichijoudani.

[[File:Honda Tenno Video.mp4|thumb|right|300px|A Scene from the 2014 [[Taisen drama]] ''Honda Tenno'']]
There are also numerous examples of his portrayal in a more neutral or historic framework, especially in [[Taisen drama]]s shown on television in Okaiken; the most notable of which is [[KTK]]'s [[Honda Shigetomo (Taisen drama)|''Honda Shigetomo'']] (1983) and ''Honda Tenno'' (2014). The 1983 series remains the definitive historical portrayal, noted for its meticulous recreation of the [[Battle of Tone]] and its faithful adaptation of the ''[[Mitsukoji]]'' chronicle. The 2014 ''Honda Tenno'' took greater creative liberties, incorporating elements of the Stone Pounding mythology and the Niō symbolism into its narrative framework. The drama's opening sequence depicts the birthmark on Shigetomo's palm — a detail drawn from the mythological tradition rather than the historical record — and its final episode frames his death at Ichijoudani as a ritual act of sealing, with visual references to the [[inoko]] ceremony and the Niō guardians. He appears in the manga series ''Tail of the Moon'', ''Kacchu no Senshi Gamu'', and Tsuji Kunio's historical fiction ''The Signore: Shogun of the Warring States''. Historical representations in video games include ''Shogun: Total War'', ''Total War: Shogun 2'', ''Throne of Darkness'', the ''Terumasa'' series, as well as ''Civilization V'' and ''Age of Empires II: The Conquerors''.

There are also more fictive portrayals, in which the figure of Shigetomo influences a story or inspires a characterization. In James Clavell's novel ''Shōgun'', the character Tadatomo is a pastiche of Shigetomo. In the film ''Sengoku Jieitai 1610'' Shigetomo is accidentally killed by time-travellers to prevent the coup of the Ii clan from happening and forcing a power vacuum with Sengoku period clans, extending the length of the warring states period. In the novel ''The Middle Way'', Shigetomo and Naotaka were transported to the future during the Battle of Ichijoudani.

The mythological dimension of Shigetomo's legacy has increasingly influenced his popular culture depictions. The epithet Iwa-no-Shogun has been adopted as the title of several manga and light novel series, and the {{nihongo|tetsu-kage|鉄蔭|iron shadow}} concept has been referenced in political thrillers that use the Sengoku period as an allegory for modern institutional critique. The annual ''Kanto'' festival at [[Ichijoudani]], with its ritual cutting of the hemp rope connecting the graves of Shigetomo and Naotaka, has been depicted in numerous films and documentaries, and has become one of the most recognizable cultural events associated with the Honda era.

[[File:Ichijoudani Kanto Festival.png|thumb|left|300px|The ''Kanto'' festival at Ichijoudani, where the hemp rope connecting the graves of Shigetomo and Naotaka is ritually cut each year]]

